## Variables & Data Types

### Theory: PHP's Type System

PHP uses **loose typing** (dynamic typing):
- Variables can change types dynamically
- Type checking happens at runtime
- Type coercion (automatic conversion) occurs
- PHP 7+ adds optional type hints

```php
<?php
// Dynamic typing
$var = 10;              // int
$var = "Hello";         // string (changed!)
$var = 3.14;            // float (changed!)
$var = true;            // boolean (changed!)

// Type coercion
$x = "5" + 3;           // "5" coerced to 5, result = 8
$y = "10 apples" + 5;   // "10 apples" coerced to 10, result = 15
$z = "abc" + 5;         // "abc" coerced to 0, result = 5
?>
```

### Primitive Data Types

**1. String**
```php
<?php
// Single quotes (literal)
$name = 'John';                 // No interpolation
$str = 'Can\'t use apostrophe'; // Escape with backslash

// Double quotes (interpolation)
$name = "Alice";
$greeting = "Hello, $name!";    // "Hello, Alice!"
$greeting = "Hello, {$name}!";  // Alternative syntax

// Heredoc (preserve formatting)
$text = <<<EOT
This is a long string
spanning multiple lines
with $variable interpolation
EOT;

// Nowdoc (no interpolation)
$text = <<<'EOT'
This is a string
without $variable interpolation
EOT;
?>
```

**2. Integer**
```php
<?php
$int1 = 123;
$int2 = -456;
$int3 = 0x1A;           // Hexadecimal
$int4 = 0b10101;        // Binary
$int5 = 1_000_000;      // Numeric separators (PHP 7.4+)

// Limits
$max_int = PHP_INT_MAX; // 9,223,372,036,854,775,807 (64-bit)
$min_int = PHP_INT_MIN; // -9,223,372,036,854,775,808

// Integer operations
echo 10 + 5;            // 15
echo 10 - 3;            // 7
echo 10 * 3;            // 30
echo 10 / 3;            // 3.3333...
echo 10 % 3;            // 1 (modulo)
echo 10 ** 3;           // 1000 (exponentiation)
?>
```

**3. Float**
```php
<?php
$float1 = 3.14;
$float2 = 2.5e3;        // 2500 (scientific notation)
$float3 = 1.23e-4;      // 0.000123

// Precision issues
$x = 0.1 + 0.2;
var_dump($x);           // float(0.30000000000000004)
// Always use === for float comparison
if (abs($x - 0.3) < 0.0001) {
    echo "Close enough";
}

// Infinity and NaN
$inf = INF;
$nan = NAN;
$div = 1 / 0;           // INF
$nan_val = 0 / 0;       // NAN

is_infinite($inf);      // true
is_nan($nan);           // true
is_finite(3.14);        // true
?>
```

**4. Boolean**
```php
<?php
$true_val = true;
$false_val = false;

// Truthy values (convert to true)
$truthy = array(1, "non-empty string", 1.5, true);

// Falsy values (convert to false)
$falsy = array(0, "", "0", false, NULL, array());

// Type casting to boolean
$x = (bool)"0";         // false (string "0" is falsy)
$y = (bool)"false";     // true (non-empty string)

// Comparison operators return boolean
$is_greater = 10 > 5;   // true
$is_equal = "5" == 5;   // true (loose comparison)
$is_identical = "5" === 5;  // false (strict comparison)
?>
```

**5. Array**
```php
<?php
// Indexed array
$colors = array("Red", "Green", "Blue");
$colors = ["Red", "Green", "Blue"];  // Short syntax (PHP 5.4+)

// Associative array
$person = array("name" => "John", "age" => 30);
$person = ["name" => "John", "age" => 30];

// Nested array
$matrix = [
    [1, 2, 3],
    [4, 5, 6],
    [7, 8, 9]
];

// Access elements
echo $colors[0];                // "Red"
echo $person["name"];           // "John"
echo $matrix[0][1];             // 2

// Check existence
isset($colors[0]);              // true
isset($colors[10]);             // false
array_key_exists("name", $person);  // true
?>
```

**6. NULL**
```php
<?php
$var = NULL;
$unset_var = null;              // Case-insensitive

// Unset variables default to NULL
unset($x);
var_dump($x);                   // NULL (with warning)

// Check for NULL
is_null($var);                  // true
$var === null;                  // Exact same (recommended)

// Empty string is not NULL
$empty_str = "";
var_dump($empty_str);           // string(0) ""
var_dump(is_null($empty_str));  // false
?>
```

