## Advanced OOP Concepts

### Magic Methods (Magic Methods provide special behavior)

```php
<?php
class Person {
    private $data = [];
    
    // __construct - called when object created
    public function __construct($name, $age) {
        $this->data['name'] = $name;
        $this->data['age'] = $age;
    }
    
    // __get - called when accessing undefined property
    public function __get($name) {
        echo "Getting $name\n";
        return $this->data[$name] ?? null;
    }
    
    // __set - called when setting undefined property
    public function __set($name, $value) {
        echo "Setting $name to $value\n";
        $this->data[$name] = $value;
    }
    
    // __isset - called when checking isset() on undefined property
    public function __isset($name) {
        return isset($this->data[$name]);
    }
    
    // __unset - called when unsetting undefined property
    public function __unset($name) {
        unset($this->data[$name]);
    }
    
    // __call - called when invoking undefined method
    public function __call($method, $args) {
        if ($method === 'getName') {
            return $this->data['name'];
        }
    }
    
    // __callStatic - called on static undefined method
    public static function __callStatic($method, $args) {
        echo "Static method $method called\n";
    }
    
    // __toString - called when object treated as string
    public function __toString() {
        return "{$this->data['name']} ({$this->data['age']})";
    }
    
    // __invoke - called when object used as function
    public function __invoke($x) {
        return $x * 2;
    }
    
    // __clone - called when object is cloned
    public function __clone() {
        echo "Object cloned\n";
    }
    
    // __debugInfo - customizes var_dump output (PHP 5.6+)
    public function __debugInfo() {
        return ['name' => $this->data['name'], 'age' => $this->data['age']];
    }
    
    // __serialize / __unserialize (PHP 7.4+)
    public function __serialize() {
        return $this->data;
    }
    
    public function __unserialize($data) {
        $this->data = $data;
    }
}

$person = new Person("Alice", 30);
echo $person;               // "Alice (30)"
echo $person->name;         // Getting name
$person->email = "alice@example.com";  // Setting email
echo $person->name;         // "Alice"
echo $person("test");       // 20 (invoke magic method, returns 20*2 - wait no, string "test"*2)
$person('name');            // Using as callable
?>
```

**Theory:** Magic methods allow intercepting property/method access, enabling:
- Lazy loading of properties
- Dynamic getters/setters
- Method overloading
- Converting objects to strings
- Making objects callable

### Abstract Classes & Methods

```php
<?php
// Abstract class - cannot be instantiated directly
abstract class Animal {
    protected $name;
    
    public function __construct($name) {
        $this->name = $name;
    }
    
    // Abstract method - must be implemented by subclass
    abstract public function sound();
    
    // Regular method - inherited
    public function getName() {
        return $this->name;
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    public function sound() {
        return "Woof!";
    }
}

// $animal = new Animal("Generic");  // Error! Can't instantiate abstract
$dog = new Dog("Rex");
echo $dog->sound();         // "Woof!"
?>
```

**Theory:** Abstract classes define a contract that subclasses must follow:
- Cannot instantiate directly
- Force implementation of specific methods
- Share common code through non-abstract methods
- Enforce structure and consistency

### Static Late Binding

```php
<?php
class A {
    public static $name = "Class A";
    
    public static function who() {
        // self:: - always refers to A
        echo self::$name;       // Always "Class A"
    }
    
    public static function whoAmI() {
        // static:: - late binding (PHP 5.3+)
        echo static::$name;     // Refers to called class
    }
}

class B extends A {
    public static $name = "Class B";
}

A::who();                   // "Class A"
B::who();                   // "Class A" (self is still A)
A::whoAmI();                // "Class A"
B::whoAmI();                // "Class B" (static binds to B)
?>
```

**Theory:** Late static binding allows subclasses to be referenced properly:
- `self::` - always the class where method defined
- `static::` - the class the method was called on
- Critical for inheritance and polymorphism

