## Architecture Patterns

### Model-View-Controller (MVC)

```php
<?php
// Model - Business logic and data
class User {
    private $db;
    
    public function __construct(Database $db) {
        $this->db = $db;
    }
    
    public function findById($id) {
        return $this->db->query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?", [$id]);
    }
    
    public function create($email, $password) {
        return $this->db->query(
            "INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES (?, ?)",
            [$email, password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT)]
        );
    }
}

// Controller - Handles requests
class UserController {
    private $userModel;
    
    public function __construct(User $userModel) {
        $this->userModel = $userModel;
    }
    
    public function show($id) {
        $user = $this->userModel->findById($id);
        
        if (!$user) {
            return $this->notFound();
        }
        
        return $this->render('user', ['user' => $user]);
    }
    
    public function create() {
        $user = $this->userModel->create(
            $_POST['email'],
            $_POST['password']
        );
        return $this->redirect('/user/' . $user->id);
    }
}

// View - Presentation
// user.php
echo "<h1>{$user['name']}</h1>";
echo "<p>{$user['email']}</p>";

// Router
$controller = new UserController($userModel);
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'GET') {
    $controller->show($_GET['id']);
} elseif ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {
    $controller->create();
}
?>
```

**Flow:** Request → Controller → Model → View → Response

### Repository Pattern

```php
<?php
// Problem: Decouple business logic from database

interface UserRepository {
    public function findById($id);
    public function findAll();
    public function save(User $user);
    public function delete($id);
}

class MySQLUserRepository implements UserRepository {
    private $db;
    
    public function __construct(Database $db) {
        $this->db = $db;
    }
    
    public function findById($id) {
        $stmt = $this->db->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?");
        $stmt->execute([$id]);
        return $stmt->fetch();
    }
    
    public function findAll() {
        return $this->db->query("SELECT * FROM users")->fetchAll();
    }
    
    public function save(User $user) {
        if ($user->id) {
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare(
                "UPDATE users SET name = ?, email = ? WHERE id = ?"
            );
            $stmt->execute([$user->name, $user->email, $user->id]);
        } else {
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare(
                "INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)"
            );
            $stmt->execute([$user->name, $user->email]);
            $user->id = $this->db->lastInsertId();
        }
        return $user;
    }
    
    public function delete($id) {
        $stmt = $this->db->prepare("DELETE FROM users WHERE id = ?");
        $stmt->execute([$id]);
    }
}

// Service layer uses repository
class UserService {
    private $userRepository;
    
    public function __construct(UserRepository $userRepository) {
        $this->userRepository = $userRepository;
    }
    
    public function getUserWithOrders($id) {
        $user = $this->userRepository->findById($id);
        // Business logic
        return $user;
    }
}

// Can easily swap implementations
class MockUserRepository implements UserRepository {
    public function findById($id) { return ['id' => 1, 'name' => 'Mock']; }
    // ...
}
?>
```

**Benefits:** 
- Database independent
- Easy testing with mocks
- Single responsibility
- Flexible implementation

### Service Locator (Anti-Pattern)

```php
<?php
// Anti-pattern: Using global service locator
class ServiceLocator {
    private static $services = [];
    
    public static function register($name, $service) {
        self::$services[$name] = $service;
    }
    
    public static function get($name) {
        return self::$services[$name] ?? null;
    }
}

// Usage - avoid this!
ServiceLocator::register('database', $db);
ServiceLocator::register('email', $emailService);

class UserService {
    public function create($user) {
        $db = ServiceLocator::get('database');  // Hidden dependency!
        $email = ServiceLocator::get('email');  // Hard to test
        // ...
    }
}

// Problems:
// - Hidden dependencies (can't see what class needs)
// - Hard to test (need to setup ServiceLocator)
// - Tight coupling to ServiceLocator
// - Runtime errors if service missing

// Better: Use dependency injection
class UserService {
    public function __construct(Database $db, EmailService $email) {
        // Dependencies visible, easy to mock for testing
    }
}
?>
```

