## Advanced String & Regex Operations

### Regular Expression Patterns

```php
<?php
// Basic pattern matching
$pattern = '/hello/i';                  // Case-insensitive
$subject = "Hello World";

preg_match($pattern, $subject);         // 1 (match found)

// Common patterns
/^\d{3}-\d{4}$/                         // XXX-XXXX format
/^[a-z0-9._%+-]+@[a-z0-9.-]+\.[a-z]{2,}$/  // Email
/^https?:\/\//                          // HTTP/HTTPS URL
/^(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*\d).{8,}$/  // Strong password

// Groups and capturing
preg_match('/(\w+)@(\w+\.\w+)/', 'john@example.com', $matches);
// $matches[0] = 'john@example.com'
// $matches[1] = 'john'
// $matches[2] = 'example.com'

// Named groups (PHP 7.3+)
preg_match('/(?<name>\w+)@(?<domain>\w+\.\w+)/', 'john@example.com', $matches);
// $matches['name'] = 'john'
// $matches['domain'] = 'example.com'

// Replace with callback
$text = "Hello John, Hello Alice";
$result = preg_replace_callback(
    '/Hello (\w+)/',
    fn($matches) => "Hi {$matches[1]}",
    $text
);
// "Hi John, Hi Alice"

// Split on pattern
$csv = "apple,banana,cherry";
$items = preg_split('/,/', $csv);
// ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]

// Find all matches
$text = "Email: john@example.com, alice@test.com";
preg_match_all('/[\w.-]+@[\w.-]+/', $text, $matches);
// $matches[0] = ["john@example.com", "alice@test.com"]

// Lookahead/Lookbehind
/(?=@)/                     // Lookahead: matches before @
/(?<=@)/                    // Lookbehind: matches after @
?>
```

**Theory:** Regular expressions are patterns for matching and manipulating strings:
- Anchors: `^` (start), `$` (end)
- Character classes: `\d` (digit), `\w` (word), `\s` (space)
- Quantifiers: `*` (0+), `+` (1+), `?` (0-1), `{n,m}` (range)
- Groups: `()` for capturing, `(?:)` for non-capturing
- Alternation: `|` for or

