### Q2: What is the difference between `==` and `===`?

**Answer:**
```php
<?php
// == (Loose comparison) - coerces types before comparison
0 == false;                     // true (0 coerced to false)
"0" == false;                   // true (string "0" coerced to false)
1 == "1";                       // true (string "1" coerced to 1)
"10" == 10;                     // true (string coerced to int)
null == false;                  // true (null coerced to false)

// === (Strict comparison) - no type coercion
0 === false;                    // false (int vs bool)
"0" === false;                  // false (string vs bool)
1 === "1";                      // false (int vs string)
10 === "10";                    // false (int vs string)
null === false;                 // false (null vs bool)

// Real-world impact
$user_id = $_GET['id'] ?? 0;
if ($user_id == 0) {           // Dangerous! "0abc" == 0 is true
    // Wrong ID!
}

if ($user_id === 0) {          // Safe! Must be exactly 0
    // Correct!
}

// Best practice: Always use ===
// Reason: Prevents unexpected type coercion bugs
?>
```

**Rule of Thumb:** Always use `===` in PHP (strict comparison)

