### Q8: What are static methods and properties? When to use?

**Answer:**
```php
<?php
// Static methods - belong to class, not instances
class Logger {
    public static function log($message) {
        echo "[LOG] " . date('Y-m-d H:i:s') . ": $message\n";
    }
}

Logger::log("User logged in");  // No object needed

// Static properties - shared across all instances
class Counter {
    private static $count = 0;
    
    public function __construct() {
        self::$count++;         // Increment shared counter
    }
    
    public static function getCount() {
        return self::$count;
    }
}

$c1 = new Counter();
$c2 = new Counter();
echo Counter::getCount();       // 2 (shared)

// Static vs Instance
class Configuration {
    private static $config = [];
    private $instance_value;
    
    public static function set($key, $value) {
        self::$config[$key] = $value;  // Shared
    }
    
    public function setInstanceValue($value) {
        $this->instance_value = $value;  // Per object
    }
}

Configuration::set('db_host', 'localhost');  // Global
$c = new Configuration();
$c->setInstanceValue('local');               // Specific to $c

// When to use static:
// ✓ Utility functions (Logger, Helper)
// ✓ Factory methods (create instances)
// ✓ Constants (Configuration)
// ✓ Singletons

// When NOT to use static:
// ✗ If you need multiple instances
// ✗ For dependency injection
// ✗ For testability (hard to mock)
// ✗ For state that changes per request

// Better alternative - dependency injection
class EmailService {
    public function send($email) { }
}

// Bad - static
class Order {
    public static function send() {
        EmailService::send();  // Tightly coupled
    }
}

// Good - injected
class Order {
    public function __construct(private EmailService $emailService) {}
    
    public function send() {
        $this->emailService->send();  // Loosely coupled
    }
}
?>
```

**Guidelines:**
- Use static for utilities and factories
- Avoid static for business logic (testability issues)
- Consider dependency injection instead

