### Q10: Explain sessions and cookies

**Answer:**
```php
<?php
// SESSIONS - Server-side storage (more secure)
session_start();

// Set session data
$_SESSION['user_id'] = 123;
$_SESSION['username'] = 'john';
$_SESSION['role'] = 'admin';

// Session ID is stored in cookie (PHPSESSID by default)
// Server stores actual data in files or database

// Retrieve session data
if (isset($_SESSION['user_id'])) {
    echo "User ID: " . $_SESSION['user_id'];
}

// Unset specific session data
unset($_SESSION['username']);

// Destroy entire session (logout)
session_destroy();

// COOKIES - Client-side storage (less secure)
setcookie("user_preference", "dark_mode", time() + 86400);  // 1 day
setcookie("language", "en", time() + 30*24*60*60);          // 30 days

// Retrieve cookie
echo $_COOKIE['user_preference'];

// Delete cookie
setcookie("user_preference", "", time() - 1);

// Session security
session_regenerate_id(true);  // Regenerate ID, delete old session
// Use after login to prevent session fixation attack

ini_set('session.cookie_httponly', 1);      // Prevent JS access
ini_set('session.cookie_secure', 1);        // HTTPS only
ini_set('session.cookie_samesite', 'Strict');  // CSRF protection

// COMPARISON TABLE
/*
Feature          Sessions        Cookies
Storage          Server          Client
Security         Higher          Lower
Size Limit       Larger          ~4KB
Lifetime         Browser close   Configurable
Data Access      Server only     Client can read
Speed            Slower          Faster
Privacy          Better          Worse
Manipulation     Hard            Easy
Visible in URL   No              No (unless appended)
*/

// Real-world example
class AuthManager {
    public static function login($user_id, $username) {
        session_regenerate_id(true);  // Security
        $_SESSION['user_id'] = $user_id;
        $_SESSION['username'] = $username;
        $_SESSION['login_time'] = time();
    }
    
    public static function isLoggedIn() {
        return isset($_SESSION['user_id']);
    }
    
    public static function logout() {
        session_destroy();
    }
}

// Usage
AuthManager::login(1, 'john');
if (AuthManager::isLoggedIn()) {
    echo "Welcome back!";
}
AuthManager::logout();
?>
```

