### Q12: Explain lazy loading and when to use it

**Answer:**
```php
<?php
// Lazy loading - load data only when needed
class User {
    private $id;
    private $posts = null;  // null = not loaded yet
    
    public function __construct($id) {
        $this->id = $id;
    }
    
    public function getPosts() {
        if ($this->posts === null) {
            // Load only when accessed
            $this->posts = Post::where('user_id', $this->id)->get();
        }
        return $this->posts;
    }
}

// Usage
$user = new User(1);
// No query yet!

echo $user->getId();    // No query
$posts = $user->getPosts();  // Query executes here!
$posts = $user->getPosts();  // No query (cached)

// Real framework (Laravel Eloquent)
$user = User::find(1);  // Query: SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1
// $user->posts not loaded yet

foreach ($user->posts as $post) {  // Query: SELECT * FROM posts WHERE user_id = 1
    echo $post->title;
}

// When to use:
// ✓ Large datasets
// ✓ Related data not always needed
// ✓ Performance optimization
// ✓ Reduce memory usage

// When NOT to use:
// ✗ If you know data will be used
// ✗ If N+1 queries will happen
// ✗ If data must be available upfront

// Better alternative - Eager loading
$users = User::with('posts')->get();  // 2 queries total
// Instead of N+1 queries with lazy loading
?>
```

